  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper level zonal (westerly) flow will be fairly persistent over \nthe coming week, keeping temperatures close to average through the\nremainder of the week. While this upper level flow will be on the\nstronger side today and tonight, winds aloft and at the surface \nwill weaken tomorrow as our upper level flow weakens and high \npressure builds in over the plains. Although the zonal flow will \nkeep a steady supply of clouds streaming overhead, any \nprecipitation will be light and largely confined to the immediate \nContinental Divide, save for a low chance of some light rain east \nof a Great Falls to Havre line on Thursday. \n\nUpper level ridging will briefly build in over the area for early\nnext week, but this ridge may quickly give way to an upper level \ntrough that would bring another cooldown early next week.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWind tonight: Winds will remain elevated through the overnight \nhours tonight, particularly along the Rocky Mountain Front where \nsome gusts in excess of 60 mph will be possible along the \nimmediate Rocky Mountain Front. There is a low risk (~10-20 \npercent) of these waves attempting to propagate a bit onto the \nplains, but the overall profile of the atmosphere is generally not\nsupportive for these to cause impacts further out on the plains. \nAs such, I have opted against a High Wind Warning for the \novernight hours tonight. Ludwig",
